{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "target_location",
      "description": "At the demonstration cycle, the policy correctly needed to preserve the lifted block's identity through subsequent exchanges, but was required to write metric coordinates without receiving calibration. Its guessed coordinates differed from the annotations, breaking PREDICTABILITY. Replace the world-coordinate representation with the fixed front-camera pixel coordinates [u,v] of the center of the target's top face at its latest resting position. At the video cycle, identify the lifted physical block, track it through every subsequent exchange, and annotate its final top-face center, not its original slot. For example, write [127,103]. During execution retain this value while the block is held or occluded. Update it when the same block is observed released at a displaced resting position; if the top face is obscured at release, retain the value until its resting center becomes visible. Do not update for another block or for apparent image motion caused by lifting. Remove the episode-specific target examples from the old rule.",
      "value_type": "other",
      "tolerance": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "button_location",
      "description": "After the final repetition and empty-hand retreat, the button could be hidden by the robot. The previous memory contained only target location and repetition count, and the policy guessed an incorrect button approach. This breaks SUFFICIENCY because the button's earlier visible location is no longer recoverable from the current images. Add button_location, holding the stationary stop button's world xy. Write [] at the video cycle, where calibration is unavailable. At the first execution cycle with a clearly visible button and calibration, write its calibrated center, for example [-0.2126,-0.0386]. Retain that value thereafter, including during occlusion and button contact. Localize the center of the raised circular button surface using approximately z=0.02 m, not the tabletop plane.",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "target_location",
      "description": "The demonstration asks you to remember the same physical block after exchanges, but provides no calibration for a metric estimate; requiring world xy there made the memory unpredictable. Instead write target_location as fixed front-camera pixel coordinates [u,v] of the target's resting top-face center. In the video, identify the block actually lifted and follow its continuous motion through all later exchanges to the final frame. Write that final center, for example [127,103], without attempting a world-coordinate conversion. During execution carry this value while the target is held or obscured. When this cycle shows the target released and its displaced resting top-face center is visible, update the pixel coordinates. If release obscures the center, retain the previous value until a later current view reveals the same resting target. Never replace the target with the block occupying its original demonstration slot."
    },
    {
      "name": "button_location",
      "description": "The button may be visible initially but occluded when the completed repetition count calls for approaching it. Without a retained location, that waypoint is not sufficiently determined. Initialize button_location to [] in the video cycle. On the first act cycle where the button is clearly visible and calibration is supplied, localize its raised circular surface center at approximately world z=0.02 m and store world [x,y], for example [-0.2126,-0.0386]. Preserve the stored coordinates through later occlusion, manipulation, and button contact. Do not infer button coordinates from a neighboring cube or overwrite the stored location with an occluded estimate."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "The demonstration-only failures occurred because the old instructions demanded calibrated world coordinates when calibration was absent. Replace all interpretations of target_location as world xy with its new meaning: the fixed front-camera pixel location of the target's latest resting top-face center. At execution, use that remembered location to identify the physical target, then use the supplied calibration and current visible top-face geometry at approximately z=0.04 m to obtain world xy and edge alignment. Do not use the pixel numbers directly as waypoint coordinates. While the target is held, continue using the existing rules based on current TCP pose and grasp geometry rather than projecting its remembered resting point as a moving target. Preserve the existing repetition counting, grasp, lift, release, retreat, and inter-repetition realignment rules.\n\nAfter the final release and empty-hand retreat, the old instruction to localize the button solely from the current images sometimes caused guesses because the arm hid it; this breaks SUFFICIENCY. Use button_location for the open-hand approach at z=0.15, roll=-pi, pitch=0, yaw=0. Populate this field from a clearly visible calibrated execution view before occlusion, as specified in the memory instruction. Use the same stored xy for closing above the button, pressing at commanded z=0.0075, and retracting to z=0.15. Retain the existing minimum-displacement safeguard for closing and the rule that low contact is followed by retraction, not repeated deeper pressing.\n\nEven when the button was visible, projecting its raised circular center onto the tabletop displaced the approach enough to miss the recorded waypoint. Replace the unspecified button localization plane with approximately z=0.02 m for the visible circular top. This localization height is distinct from the commanded pressing height. Localize the circular button itself, not a neighboring cube or an arbitrary point on its base. No additional waiting or task-stage memory is needed."
}

